                <head>109. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On July 22, William Wieland, in a memorandum to Assistant Secretary Rubottom,
                    recalled the major developments in the U.S. Government’s policy of cutting back
                    on arms shipments to the Batista government and pointed out that the Military
                    Assistance Advisory Group in Havana had asked the Department of State to approve
                    the sale to Cuba of two items it defined as noncombat equipment. The two items
                    were both aircraft parts, which Wieland argued could not be considered as
                    noncombat items. In response to Wieland’s request for his views, Rubottom wrote
                    in the margin: “Agree.”</p>
                <p>Wieland recommended that the Department approve only those pending items that
                    clearly fell within the definition of “noncombat equipment,” such as a fire
                    truck, a 2½–ton trailer, communications equipment, telescopes, and spare parts
                    for a C–47 cargo aircraft. He emphasized that spare parts for military aircraft
                    should not be approved. Wieland said that if Rubottom accepted his
                    recommendation, he would take the necessary steps to approve the items for
                    shipment. In the margin Rubottom wrote: “Approve. RRR.” (Department of State,
                    Central Files, 737.56/7–2258)</p>
            </div>
